BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:supporting-act-foundation-artist-grant-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261008T193437Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Supporting Act Foundation: Artist Grant 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Supporting Act Foundation\, the charitable foundation estab
 lished by WeTransfer\, awards ten unrestricted Artist Grants of EUR 10\,00
 0 to emerging artists from underrepresented groups whose work involves col
 laborative\, collective or creative processes that benefit their communiti
 es. The grant is paid as EUR 1\,000 per month over one year and can be use
 d freely\; applicants should explain how it would help them overcome econo
 mic\, social\, cultural\, physical or health barriers to their practice. E
 LIGIBILITY: identify as an emerging artist with at least two and no more t
 han ten years of professional experience\; identify as belonging to an und
 errepresented group or face systemic barriers to your career\; be born in 
 or before 2005\; be based in Belgium\, France\, Germany\, Greece\, Ireland
 \, Italy\, the Netherlands\, Portugal\, Spain or the United Kingdom with a
  personal IBAN bank account there\; and have an updated website or public 
 social media profile. Former and current TSAF grantees cannot apply. TIMEL
 INE: applications close 1 October 2026 at 11:00 CET\, notifications go to 
 all applicants in late October 2026\, shortlisted applicants get a final r
 eview in November 2026\, and the public announcement and first transfer fo
 llow in January 2027. APPLICATION: free\, via the open call platform on th
 e foundation's website. The parallel Impact Grant strand for arts nonprofi
 ts runs on the same deadline.\n\nOrganisation: The Supporting Act Foundati
 on\nLocation: Remote\; for artists based in Belgium\, France\, Germany\, G
 reece\, Ireland\, Italy\, the Netherlands\, Portugal\, Spain or the United
  Kingdom.\nAward: 10 unrestricted grants of EUR 10\,000 each\, paid out at
  EUR 1\,000 per month over one year\, with the first installment transferr
 ed in January 2027. No application fee.\nDuration: One year\, from January
  2027.\n\nMore: https://thesupportingact.org/programs/artist-grant/2026?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://thesupportingact.org/programs/artist-grant/2026?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\; for artists based in Belgium\, France\, Germany\, Greece\
 , Ireland\, Italy\, the Netherlands\, Portugal\, Spain or the United Kingd
 om.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:supporting-act-foundation-impact-grant-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261008T193437Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Supporting Act Foundation: Impact Grant 2026 for Arts
  Nonprofits
DESCRIPTION:The Supporting Act Foundation\, the charitable foundation estab
 lished by WeTransfer\, awards ten unrestricted Impact Grants of EUR 50\,00
 0 (EUR 25\,000 per year over two years) to arts-focused nonprofits that em
 power emerging artists from underrepresented groups through non-financial 
 support: improving artists' living conditions\, enabling practice developm
 ent\, increasing the visibility of their work and building their confidenc
 e. ELIGIBILITY: nonprofits registered as arts organizations between 2016 a
 nd 2024\, based in Belgium\, France\, Germany\, Greece\, Ireland\, Italy\,
  the Netherlands\, Portugal\, Spain or the United Kingdom\, or working wit
 h a fiscal host in one of these countries\; annual expenditures between EU
 R 50\,000 and EUR 300\,000\; an active website demonstrating supported pro
 jects\; and an IBAN bank account. Individuals\, commercial entities\, curr
 ent or past TSAF grantees\, and proposals for ongoing projects rather than
  established organizations are not eligible. TIMELINE: applications close 
 1 October 2026 at 11:00 CET\, are reviewed in October\, notifications go o
 ut in late October 2026\, shortlisted organizations get a final review in 
 November 2026\, and the public announcement and first installment follow i
 n January 2027. APPLICATION: free\, via the open call platform on the foun
 dation's website. The parallel Artist Grant strand for individual emerging
  artists runs on the same deadline.\n\nOrganisation: The Supporting Act Fo
 undation\nLocation: Remote\; for nonprofits registered in Belgium\, France
 \, Germany\, Greece\, Ireland\, Italy\, the Netherlands\, Portugal\, Spain
  or the United Kingdom\, or working with a fiscal host there.\nAward: 10 u
 nrestricted grants of EUR 50\,000 each\, paid as EUR 25\,000 per year over
  two years\, with the first transfer in January 2027. No application fee.\
 nDuration: Two years\, from January 2027.\n\nMore: https://thesupportingac
 t.org/programs/impact-grant/2026?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm
 _medium=referral
URL:https://thesupportingact.org/programs/impact-grant/2026?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\; for nonprofits registered in Belgium\, France\, Germany\,
  Greece\, Ireland\, Italy\, the Netherlands\, Portugal\, Spain or the Unit
 ed Kingdom\, or working with a fiscal host there.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:asymmetry-assistant-curator-fellowship-nottingham-2027@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261008T193437Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261025
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261026
SUMMARY:Deadline: Asymmetry Assistant Curator Fellowship 2027-2029 at Notti
 ngham Contemporary
DESCRIPTION:Asymmetry Art Foundation and Nottingham Contemporary offer a fu
 lly funded two-year Assistant Curator Fellowship embedded in the curatoria
 l team of one of Europe's leading contemporary art institutions. The fello
 wship is geared towards an early-career curator who\, to any extent\, iden
 tifies with Chinese or Sinophone cultures or backgrounds\, continuing Asym
 metry's programme of curatorial fellowships at UK institutions (previous p
 lacements include Chisenhale Gallery and Whitechapel Gallery). WHAT YOU DO
 : contribute to exhibitions\, artist commissions\, live programmes\, resea
 rch\, writing and institutional development\, working across all stages of
  projects while developing independent curatorial work and joining Asymmet
 ry's international network of scholars and practitioners. WHAT YOU GET: an
  annual salary of GBP 27\,556 plus pension and National Insurance contribu
 tions\, annual leave plus UK bank holidays\, visa and legal fee assistance
  where required\, and a travel and relocation allowance. ELIGIBILITY: earl
 y-career curators with roughly 1 to 3 years of experience\, an educational
  background in art history\, cultural studies or related fields\, familiar
 ity with contemporary artistic practices\, direct experience collaborating
  with artists\, and proficiency in English. Applications must be in Englis
 h\; accessibility support is available via fellowship@asymmetryart.org. AP
 PLICATION: via the Asymmetry application portal. DEADLINE: 25 October 2026
 . The fellowship runs 6 April 2027 to 5 April 2029.\n\nOrganisation: Asymm
 etry Art Foundation\, with Nottingham Contemporary\nLocation: Nottingham C
 ontemporary\, Nottingham\, UK. Open internationally to early-career curato
 rs who identify with Chinese or Sinophone cultures or backgrounds\; visa a
 nd relocation support provided.\nAward: Annual salary of GBP 27\,556 for t
 wo years\, plus pension and National Insurance contributions\, annual leav
 e in line with organisational policy plus UK bank holidays\, visa and lega
 l fee assistance where required\, and a travel and relocation allowance. N
 o application fee.\nDuration: Two years\, 6 April 2027 to 5 April 2029.\n\
 nMore: https://www.asymmetryart.org/fellowships/open-calls/2027-asymmetry-
 assistant-curator-fellowship-nottingham-contemporary-opencall?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.asymmetryart.org/fellowships/open-calls/2027-asymmetry-assi
 stant-curator-fellowship-nottingham-contemporary-opencall?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Nottingham Contemporary\, Nottingham\, UK. Open internationally to
  early-career curators who identify with Chinese or Sinophone cultures or 
 backgrounds\; visa and relocation support provided.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fluxus-cpga-prize-frieze-london-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261008T193437Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260928
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Deadline: Fluxus-CPGA Prize at Frieze London 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Fluxus-CPGA Prize at Frieze London\, a collaboration betwee
 n Fluxus Art Projects\, the Comite Professionnel des Galeries d'Art and Fr
 ieze London\, supports talent from the French art scene and promotes its v
 isibility internationally. It also celebrates the relationship between art
 ists and their galleries: the GBP 15\,000 prize is shared between the two.
  WHO APPLIES: galleries of any nationality exhibiting at Frieze London 202
 6\, presenting the work of one French or French-based artist on their stan
 d\; artists cannot apply directly. HOW TO APPLY: via the Google Form linke
 d from the apply page\, with gallery contact information\, the artist's na
 me and number of works presented\, an artist biography (200 words)\, a gal
 lery description (200 words) and 1 to 2 images. Free of charge. Previous l
 aureates include Daiga Grantina (Emalin\, 2025)\, Stephanie Saade (Marfa' 
 Projects\, 2024) and Josefa Ntjam (Nicoletti\, 2023)\; jurors have include
 d Hans Ulrich Obrist\, Zoe Whitley\, Catherine Wood and Valeria Napoleone.
  Deadline 28 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Fluxus Art Projects\, with t
 he Comite Professionnel des Galeries d'Art and Frieze London\nLocation: Fr
 ieze London\, Regent's Park\, London\, UK (October 2026). Galleries of any
  nationality can apply\, provided they present the work of a French or Fre
 nch-based artist on their stand.\nAward: GBP 15\,000 prize\, shared betwee
 n the artist and their gallery. Participation is free of charge.\nDuration
 : Awarded during Frieze London in October 2026\n\nMore: https://fluxusartp
 rojects.com/apply?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
URL:https://fluxusartprojects.com/apply?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Frieze London\, Regent's Park\, London\, UK (October 2026). Galler
 ies of any nationality can apply\, provided they present the work of a Fre
 nch or French-based artist on their stand.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fluxus-prize-art-basel-paris-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261008T193437Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260929
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260930
SUMMARY:Deadline: Fluxus Prize at Art Basel Paris 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Fluxus Prize at Art Basel Paris\, from Fluxus Art Projects\
 , supports talent from the British art scene and promotes its visibility i
 nternationally\, honouring the relationship between artists and their gall
 eries: the EUR 15\,000 prize is shared between the two. This edition is su
 pported by Belinda de Gaudemar and Laurent Issaurat. WHO APPLIES: gallerie
 s of any nationality exhibiting at Art Basel Paris 2026 in the Galleries o
 r Premise sectors\, presenting the work of one British or UK-based artist\
 ; artists cannot apply directly. HOW TO APPLY: by email to visual.arts[at]
 institut-francais.org.uk with gallery contact information\, the artist's n
 ame and number of works presented\, an artist biography (200 words)\, a ga
 llery description (200 words) and 1 to 2 images. Free of charge. Previous 
 laureates include Olu Ogunnaike (Hollybush Gardens\, 2025) and Jesse Darli
 ng (Sultana\, 2024)\; jurors have included Hans Ulrich Obrist\, Caroline B
 ourgeois and Daria de Beauvais. Deadline 29 September 2026.\n\nOrganisatio
 n: Fluxus Art Projects\, with Art Basel Paris\nLocation: Art Basel Paris\,
  France (October 2026). Galleries of any nationality can apply\, provided 
 they present the work of one British or UK-based artist in the Galleries o
 r Premise sectors.\nAward: EUR 15\,000 prize\, shared between the artist a
 nd their gallery. Participation is free of charge.\nDuration: Awarded duri
 ng Art Basel Paris in October 2026\n\nMore: https://fluxusartprojects.com/
 apply?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://fluxusartprojects.com/apply?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Art Basel Paris\, France (October 2026). Galleries of any national
 ity can apply\, provided they present the work of one British or UK-based 
 artist in the Galleries or Premise sectors.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iaps-ai-policy-fellowship-spring-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261008T193437Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260927
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260928
SUMMARY:Deadline: IAPS: AI Policy Fellowship Spring 2027 (DC\, London or re
 mote)
DESCRIPTION:The Institute for AI Policy and Strategy (IAPS) runs a three-mo
 nth\, fully funded AI policy fellowship placing fellows on concrete AI gov
 ernance and policy research projects. Spring 2027 cohort: 22 February to 1
 4 May 2027\, opening with a mandatory in-person component in Washington DC
  from 22 February to 5 March 2027\, after which fellows work from the DC o
 ffice\, the London office or remotely. STIPENDS: USD 18\,000 for Fellows a
 nd USD 24\,000 for Senior Fellows\, plus USD 3\,000 extra on the in-person
  DC or London tracks\, healthcare benefits and a USD 500 professional deve
 lopment stipend. ELIGIBILITY: no technical background or prior policy expe
 rience required\; the DC track requires the right to work in the US betwee
 n 22 February and 14 May 2027\, and no visa sponsorship is available for t
 he US or UK\; the remote track is open globally with country-specific exce
 ptions. TIMELINE: initial responses mid-October 2026\, live interviews in 
 November\, final selections early December. Deadline 27 September 2026. Ap
 ply at https://www.guidedtrack.com/programs/ll4vpg7/run.\n\nOrganisation: 
 Institute for AI Policy and Strategy (IAPS)\nLocation: Washington DC or Lo
 ndon track (desk space provided)\, or fully remote from most countries. Ma
 ndatory in-person opening in Washington DC\, 22 February to 5 March 2027. 
 No visa sponsorship for the US or UK.\nAward: Fellows receive USD 18\,000 
 and Senior Fellows USD 24\,000 as a stipend for the three-month full-time 
 program\, plus an additional USD 3\,000 stipend on the in-person DC or Lon
 don tracks\, healthcare benefits and a USD 500 professional development st
 ipend. Up to 45 fellows. No application fee.\nDuration: Three months full-
 time\, 22 February to 14 May 2027\; extensions of 4 to 12 weeks were offer
 ed to more than half of the previous cohort\n\nMore: https://www.iaps.ai/f
 ellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.iaps.ai/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington DC or London track (desk space provided)\, or fully rem
 ote from most countries. Mandatory in-person opening in Washington DC\, 22
  February to 5 March 2027. No visa sponsorship for the US or UK.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tpg-future-of-archives-whose-archive-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261008T193437Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Photographers' Gallery: Future of Archives\, Whose Ar
 chive is it Anyway? (Call for Speakers)
DESCRIPTION:Whose Archive is it Anyway? is the first in a three-part series
  of events at The Photographers' Gallery exploring the future of archives 
 and archiving. This one-day event on Friday 16 April 2027 kickstarts the c
 onversation with commissioned talks addressing what it means to make an ar
 chive today\, who decides what an archive consists of\, who creates an arc
 hive\, who an archive is for\, and who has access. The gallery welcomes su
 bmissions of real-life experiences rather than academic papers that challe
 nge or answer these or other points related to the future of archives. SUG
 GESTED TOPICS: artist and photographers' legacies\; institutional archives
 \; memory (forgotten or lost histories made visible again)\; truth within 
 archives (who is telling the stories and how)\; ownership (who has the aut
 hority to tell stories\, who owns the images and their stories)\; relation
 ships between those who create archives and those who use them\; radical\,
  non-traditional archives that redesign how stories are gathered and held 
 to safeguard living memories\; family archives\; reclaimed photographic ar
 chives\; counter archives that question dominant historical narratives\; g
 rassroots archives\; technologies that reduce barriers to archives while s
 afeguarding material\; digital preservation and digitisation\; and dialogi
 c archives. ELIGIBILITY: open to all\, especially those with experience of
  working with archives and the stories within them\; applicants must be 18
  or older and available to attend The Photographers' Gallery in London on 
 16 April 2027 to speak at the event. Selected speakers receive a fee of GB
 P 200 and will be notified by 18 January 2027. Questions to archive@tpg.or
 g.uk. Deadline 1 November 2026\, 11:59pm GMT.\n\nOrganisation: The Photogr
 aphers' Gallery\nLocation: London\, UK. Selected speakers must be availabl
 e to speak in person at The Photographers' Gallery on 16 April 2027.\nAwar
 d: GBP 200 speaker fee for each selected speaker. No application fee.\nDur
 ation: One-day event on Friday 16 April 2027\n\nMore: https://forms.cloud.
 microsoft/pages/responsepage.aspx?id=Sykm7fNJbU-mcP8tt-A2n-2SkeDTc21FsI7Zw
 hvDxVtUNTc3RVExTjRRUDRUSVU5MzUzWDMwMlBQMC4u&route=shorturl&utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://forms.cloud.microsoft/pages/responsepage.aspx?id=Sykm7fNJbU-mcP
 8tt-A2n-2SkeDTc21FsI7ZwhvDxVtUNTc3RVExTjRRUDRUSVU5MzUzWDMwMlBQMC4u&route=s
 horturl&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:London\, UK. Selected speakers must be available to speak in perso
 n at The Photographers' Gallery on 16 April 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:delfina-foundation-curators-central-west-africa-2027@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20261008T193437Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Delfina Foundation: Open Call for Curators Based in Centr
 al and West Africa (London residency)
DESCRIPTION:With support from Delfina Foundation's Network of Patrons for A
 frica\, Delfina is offering a six- or twelve-week London residency within 
 its winter 2027 season to curators based in Central and West Africa. ELIGI
 BILITY: applicants must hold nationality and be based in one of Angola\, B
 enin\, Burkina Faso\, Burundi\, Cabo Verde\, Cameroon\, Central African Re
 public\, Chad\, Cote d'Ivoire\, Democratic Republic of the Congo\, Equator
 ial Guinea\, Gabon\, Ghana\, Guinea\, Guinea-Bissau\, Liberia\, Mali\, Mau
 ritania\, Niger\, Nigeria\, Republic of the Congo\, Sao Tome and Principe\
 , Senegal\, Sierra Leone\, The Gambia or Togo\; and must have at least fiv
 e years of professional curatorial practice in a freelance\, academic or i
 nstitutional context. THE PROGRAMME: alongside time and space to develop t
 heir work\, residents get guided visits to museums\, galleries\, non-profi
 ts\, artist-run spaces and studios\, plus trips to institutions outside Lo
 ndon\; opportunities to meet and engage with artists\, curators\, academic
 s and researchers through organised presentations\, studio visits\, events
  and meals\; professional development including presentations and crits\, 
 portfolio reviews and mentoring activities to strengthen their practice\; 
 and collaborative exchange between residents through informal gatherings a
 nd meals. APPLY via the application form on the open call page\; Delfina s
 trongly recommends reading the Guidance Notes document carefully before st
 arting. Deadline Sunday 30 August 2026\, 23:59 BST.\n\nOrganisation: Delfi
 na Foundation\nLocation: Delfina Foundation\, 29-31 Catherine Place\, Lond
 on\, UK. Applicants must hold nationality and be based in an eligible Cent
 ral or West African country.\nAward: Fully supported: a return economy-cla
 ss flight\, airport transfers in the UK\, reimbursement of visa fees if re
 quired\, a living allowance of GBP 40 per day\, a materials and research a
 llowance of GBP 70 per week\, a travel pass for London's public transport 
 network\, and a single bedroom with a desk at Delfina Foundation\, a house
  hosting up to eight residents with shared bathrooms\, laundry rooms\, kit
 chen\, library and dining room. Supported by Delfina Foundation's Network 
 of Patrons for Africa. No application fee.\nDuration: Six or twelve weeks 
 within Delfina's winter 2027 season\, which runs 5 January to 28 March 202
 7.\n\nMore: https://www.delfinafoundation.com/open-calls/current/open-call
 -for-curators-based-in-central-and-west-africa/?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.delfinafoundation.com/open-calls/current/open-call-for-cura
 tors-based-in-central-and-west-africa/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Delfina Foundation\, 29-31 Catherine Place\, London\, UK. Applican
 ts must hold nationality and be based in an eligible Central or West Afric
 an country.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:delfina-gulbenkian-artists-portugal-lusophone-africa-2027@artificialnou
 veau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261008T193437Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261004
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261005
SUMMARY:Deadline: Delfina Foundation x Calouste Gulbenkian Foundation: Open
  Call for Artists from Portugal and Portuguese-Speaking African Nations (L
 ondon residency)
DESCRIPTION:In partnership with the Calouste Gulbenkian Foundation (UK Bran
 ch)\, Delfina Foundation welcomes applications from contemporary visual ar
 tists from Portugal\, Angola\, Mozambique\, Guinea-Bissau\, Cape Verde and
  Sao Tome and Principe for a London residency from 28 June to 17 September
  2027\, during Delfina's open summer season dedicated to independent resea
 rch. ELIGIBILITY: applicants must be born in one of the six eligible count
 ries (individuals currently based in the UK are not eligible)\; be seeking
  to develop their practice through research\; be able to explain how an in
 ternational residency may benefit their career development and local netwo
 rks\; and be proficient in English (the interview is conducted in English)
 . Collectives and duos are advised to put forth one member to be in reside
 nce. THE PROGRAMME: alongside time and space to develop their work\, resid
 ents get guided visits to museums\, galleries\, non-profits\, artist-run s
 paces and studios\, plus trips to institutions outside London\; opportunit
 ies to meet artists\, curators\, academics and researchers through organis
 ed presentations\, studio visits\, events and meals\; professional develop
 ment including presentations and crits\, portfolio reviews and mentoring\;
  and collaborative exchange between residents through informal gatherings 
 and meals. APPLY via the online application form (Cognito Forms\, save-and
 -resume supported)\; Delfina strongly recommends reading the Guidance Note
 s document carefully before starting. All applicants notified by mid-Novem
 ber 2026. Deadline Sunday 4 October 2026\, 23:59 BST.\n\nOrganisation: Del
 fina Foundation\, in partnership with the Calouste Gulbenkian Foundation (
 UK Branch)\nLocation: Delfina Foundation\, 29-31 Catherine Place\, London\
 , UK. Applicants must be born in Portugal\, Angola\, Mozambique\, Guinea-B
 issau\, Cape Verde or Sao Tome and Principe\; individuals currently based 
 in the UK are not eligible.\nAward: Twelve-week fully funded residency: a 
 return economy-class flight\, airport transfers in the UK\, reimbursement 
 of visa fees if required\, a living allowance of GBP 40 per day\, a materi
 als budget of GBP 1\,200\, a travel pass for London's public transport net
 work\, and a single bedroom with a desk at Delfina Foundation\, a house ho
 sting up to eight residents with shared bathrooms\, laundry rooms\, kitche
 n\, library and dining room. No application fee.\nDuration: Twelve weeks\,
  28 June to 17 September 2027\, during Delfina's open summer 2027 residenc
 y season.\n\nMore: https://www.delfinafoundation.com/open-calls/current/op
 en-call-for-artists-from-portugal-or-portugese-speaking-african-nations/?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.delfinafoundation.com/open-calls/current/open-call-for-arti
 sts-from-portugal-or-portugese-speaking-african-nations/?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Delfina Foundation\, 29-31 Catherine Place\, London\, UK. Applican
 ts must be born in Portugal\, Angola\, Mozambique\, Guinea-Bissau\, Cape V
 erde or Sao Tome and Principe\; individuals currently based in the UK are 
 not eligible.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:innovate-uk-next-wave-createch-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261008T193437Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260811
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260812
SUMMARY:Deadline: Innovate UK: Next Wave Breakthrough Wave 1\, Createch (20
 26)
DESCRIPTION:Next Wave Breakthrough Wave 1 is Innovate UK's current open Cre
 atech competition\, investing up to GBP 10 million in early-stage innovati
 on at the intersection of creative technology and the frontier industries 
 named in the Industrial Strategy: video games\, advertising and marketing\
 , film and television\, and music and the performing and visual arts. It f
 unds industrial research to take innovations beyond proof of concept and d
 e-risk future investment\, with individual projects between GBP 100\,000 a
 nd 500\,000 in total costs. Grant rates depend on organisation size\, from
  70 percent for micro and small enterprises down to 50 percent for large o
 rganisations\, so applicants must supply match funding for the remainder. 
 The lead must be a UK-registered SME\; non-profits and CICs are barred fro
 m leading\, though they can participate as collaborators alongside a comme
 rcial lead. Non-funded partners may be based internationally. The competit
 ion opened 23 June 2026 and closes 11 August 2026 at 11:00am UK time. NOTE
  ON FIT: this is an R&D competition for creative-technology product develo
 pment\, not a fund for delivering festivals or exhibitions\, so it suits a
 n organisation whose work can be framed as industrial research with a comm
 ercial SME lead.\n\nOrganisation: Innovate UK\, UK Research and Innovation
 \nLocation: United Kingdom. Project work must be carried out in the UK\, r
 esults exploited in the UK\, and most funding spent in the UK.\nAward: GBP
  100\,000 to 500\,000 total project costs from a GBP 10 million fund\; no 
 organisation may receive more than GBP 3 million across the programme. Gra
 nt intervention rates for industrial research: up to 70 percent for micro 
 and small enterprises\, 60 percent for medium\, 50 percent for large. Rese
 arch organisations doing non-economic work can claim up to 100 percent\, o
 r 80 percent of full economic cost for academic institutions. The balance 
 is match funding. No application fee.\nDuration: 9 to 12 months\; projects
  must start by 1 January 2027 and end by 31 December 2027.\n\nMore: https:
 //apply-for-innovation-funding.service.gov.uk/competition/2517/overview/6f
 f2066d-4639-44e6-bb4f-ce7099222739?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://apply-for-innovation-funding.service.gov.uk/competition/2517/ov
 erview/6ff2066d-4639-44e6-bb4f-ce7099222739?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United Kingdom. Project work must be carried out in the UK\, resul
 ts exploited in the UK\, and most funding spent in the UK.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ahrc-design-generators-round-two-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261008T193437Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260929
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260930
SUMMARY:Deadline: AHRC: Design Generators Round Two (2026)
DESCRIPTION:Design Generators round two funds design-led research projects 
 combining design interventions with arts and humanities methodologies to c
 ontribute to the green transition. Eligible activity includes design resea
 rch prototyping\, circular business model exploration\, strengthening thir
 d-sector resilience\, skills development\, policy and strategy work\, and 
 public engagement programmes\, so exhibition-style and public-facing outpu
 ts are in scope where they sit inside a research project. Awards are GBP 1
 50\,000 to 200\,000 at 100 percent full economic cost with AHRC paying 80 
 percent\, meaning the host organisation carries a 20 percent institutional
  contribution. The lead must be a postdoctoral-standing researcher at an A
 HRC-eligible UK research organisation\, and every project must pair that l
 ead with at least one non-academic co-lead from business\, the public sect
 or\, the third sector or a community organisation\, which is the practical
  entry point for an arts organisation. Freelancers and independent consult
 ants cannot lead. The opportunity opened 21 July 2026 and closes 29 Septem
 ber 2026 at 4:00pm UK time. Decision lag is roughly three months from the 
 deadline. IMPORTANT: this round explicitly excludes international stakehol
 ders and international activity\, so non-UK partners cannot be involved.\n
 \nOrganisation: Arts and Humanities Research Council (AHRC)\, UK Research 
 and Innovation\nLocation: United Kingdom. Lead applicant must be employed 
 by\, or hold a formal written arrangement with\, a UK research organisatio
 n eligible for AHRC funding. Project activities must take place in the UK.
 \nAward: GBP 150\,000 to 200\,000 per project at 100 percent full economic
  cost\; AHRC funds 80 percent and the host research organisation must cove
 r the remaining 20 percent. Total fund GBP 1\,000\,000\, approximately 5 a
 wards. Covers research staff time\, design prototyping\, partner costs and
  public engagement activity. Subcontractors and PhD studentships are not f
 undable. No application fee.\nDuration: 9 to 12 months\, with projects req
 uired to start by 1 February 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.ukri.org/opportuni
 ty/design-generators-round-two/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_
 medium=referral
URL:https://www.ukri.org/opportunity/design-generators-round-two/?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United Kingdom. Lead applicant must be employed by\, or hold a for
 mal written arrangement with\, a UK research organisation eligible for AHR
 C funding. Project activities must take place in the UK.
END:VEVENT
